Since the diode is ideal, it conducts only during the positive half cycle. The total resistance in the circuit is \[R_{\text{eq}}
=
100+200+300
=
600~\Omega.\] The source voltage is \(v(t)=60\cos(314t).\) Hence, during the positive half cycle, the current is \[i(t)
=
\frac{60\cos(314t)}{600}
=
\frac{\cos(314t)}{10}
\quad
\text{A}.\] The average power dissipated in the \(300~\Omega\) resistor is \[P
=
\frac{1}{T}
\int_{0}^{T/2}
i^2(t)\,(300)\,dt.\] Substituting \(i(t)\), \[P
=
\frac{300}{T}
\int_{0}^{T/2}
\left(\frac{\cos(314t)}{10}\right)^2
dt.\] Using the identity \[\cos^2\theta
=
\frac{1+\cos2\theta}{2},\] we get \[\frac{1}{T}
\int_{0}^{T/2}
\cos^2(314t)\,dt
=
\frac{1}{2T}
\int_{0}^{T/2}
\left(1+\cos(628t)\right)dt\] \[=
\frac{1}{2T}
\left[
t+\frac{\sin(628t)}{628}
\right]_{0}^{T/2}\] Since \[314=\omega,
\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi}{314},\] we have \[628\left(\frac{T}{2}\right)
=
2\pi,\] hence \(\sin(2\pi)=\sin(0)=0\) Therefore, \[\frac{1}{T}
\int_{0}^{T/2}
\cos^2(314t)\,dt
=
\frac{1}{2T}
\left(\frac{T}{2}\right)
=
\frac{1}{4}.\] we get \[P
=
300
\left(\frac{1}{100}\right)
\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)
=
0.75~\text{W}.\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{C. }0.75~\text{W}}\]

For an instrumentation amplifier, \[V_{o}=A_{d}(V_{1}-V_{2})+A_{cm}V_{cm},\] where \[V_{cm}=\frac{V_{1}+V_{2}}{2}.\] The given output is \[V_{o}=100(V_{1}-V_{2})+10^{-4}(V_{1}+V_{2}).\] Comparing with the standard expression, \[A_{d}=100,\] and \[A_{cm}V_{cm}
=10^{-4}(V_{1}+V_{2})
=2\times10^{-4}\left(\frac{V_{1}+V_{2}}{2}\right).\] Hence, \[A_{cm}=2\times10^{-4}.\] Therefore, \[\mathrm{CMRR}
=\frac{A_{d}}{A_{cm}}
=\frac{100}{2\times10^{-4}}
=5\times10^{5}.\] In decibels, \[\mathrm{CMRR}_{\mathrm{dB}}
=20\log_{10}(5\times10^{5})
\approx20(5.699)
\approx114\,\text{dB}
\approx120\,\text{dB}.\] Thus, \[A_{d}=100,\qquad
\mathrm{CMRR}\approx120\,\text{dB}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }100, 120\,\text{dB}}\]

The circuit consists of two op-amp stages driving the load \(R_{L}\). <img src="img/86cb0d378acb0330.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> <strong>Upper op-amp:</strong><br/>It is a non-inverting amplifier with \[R_{f}=3R,\qquad R_{1}=R.\] Hence, \[V_{o1}
=\left(1+\frac{3R}{R}\right)V_{i}
=4V_{i}.\] <strong>Lower op-amp:</strong><br/>It is an inverting amplifier with \[R_{f}=4R,\qquad R_{1}=R.\] Therefore, \[V_{o2}
=-\frac{4R}{R}V_{i}
=-4V_{i}.\] The load \(R_{L}\) is connected between the two outputs, and the output voltage is measured as \[V_{o}=V_{o1}-V_{o2}.\] Thus, \[V_{o}
=4V_{i}-(-4V_{i})
=8V_{i}.\] Hence, \[\frac{V_{o}}{V_{i}}=8.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }8}\]

For the given op-amp operating with negative feedback, \[V_{-}=V_{+}.\] The non-inverting terminal is connected to a voltage divider formed by \(30\,\text{k}\Omega\) and \(20\,\text{k}\Omega\). Hence, \[V_{+}
=\frac{20}{30+20}V_{o}
=0.4V_{o}.\] Therefore, \[V_{-}=0.4V_{o}.\] Applying KCL at the inverting input node, \[0.3\,\text{mA}
=
\frac{V_{-}-V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Substituting \(V_{-}=0.4V_{o}\), \[0.3\times10^{-3}
=
\frac{0.4V_{o}-V_{o}}{10\times10^{3}},\] \[0.3\times10^{-3}
=
\frac{-0.6V_{o}}{10\times10^{3}}.\] Hence, \[V_{o}
=
-\frac{0.3\times10^{-3}\times10\times10^{3}}{0.6}
=-5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a)}-5\,\text{V}}\]

The voltage at the non-inverting terminal is obtained using the voltage divider: \[V_{+}
=\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{10\,\text{k}\Omega+10\,\text{k}\Omega}\times3
=1.5\,\text{V}.\] Since the op-amp is operating with negative feedback, \[V_{-}=V_{+}=1.5\,\text{V}.\] Applying KCL at the inverting terminal, \[\frac{1.5-3}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{1.5-V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Simplifying, \[1.5-3+1.5-V_{o}=0,\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }0\,\text{V}}\]

The circuit is a <strong>non-inverting Schmitt trigger</strong>. The inverting terminal is grounded, while the non-inverting terminal receives the input through \(5\,\text{k}\Omega\) and positive feedback through \(10\,\text{k}\Omega\). <img src="img/7af33a1c4caeafe3.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Applying KCL at the non-inverting input, \[\frac{V_{i}-V_{+}}{5\,\text{k}\Omega}
+
\frac{V_{o}-V_{+}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Since the op-amp switches when \[V_{+}=V_{-}=0,\] the threshold voltage becomes \[\frac{V_{i}}{5\,\text{k}\Omega}
+
\frac{V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Hence, \[V_{i}=-\frac{5}{10}V_{o}
=-\frac{V_{o}}{2}.\] <strong>Upper Threshold (UTP):</strong><br/>Before switching, the output is at negative saturation, \[V_{o}=-15\,\text{V}.\] Therefore, \[V_{TH}
=-\frac{-10}{2}
=+5\,\text{V}.\] <strong>Lower Threshold (LTP):</strong><br/>Before switching, the output is at positive saturation, \[V_{o}=+10\,\text{V}.\] Hence, \[V_{TL}
=-\frac{10}{2}
=-5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }V_{TL}=-5\,\text{V},\;V_{TH}=+5\,\text{V}}\]

The circuit is an <strong>active band-pass filter</strong>. <img src="img/2dc46309f766b4da.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> <strong>Lower cut-off frequency:</strong><br/>At low frequencies, capacitor \(C_{1}\) behaves as an open circuit. Hence, the input network acts as a high-pass filter with cutoff frequency \[\omega_{L}=\frac{1}{R_{1}C_{1}}.\] <strong>Higher cut-off frequency:</strong><br/>At high frequencies, capacitor \(C_{2}\) behaves as a short circuit, reducing the feedback impedance. Thus, the feedback network acts as a low-pass filter with cutoff frequency \[\omega_{H}=\frac{1}{R_{2}C_{2}}.\] Therefore, the circuit passes frequencies between \(\omega_{L}\) and \(\omega_{H}\). \[\boxed{\omega_{L}=\frac{1}{R_{1}C_{1}},\qquad
\omega_{H}=\frac{1}{R_{2}C_{2}}}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) Band-pass filter with }
\omega_{L}=\frac{1}{R_{1}C_{1}},\;
\omega_{H}=\frac{1}{R_{2}C_{2}}}\]

Let the forward-path plant be \[G(s)=\frac{1}{s(1+5s)}\] For finding the error due to the disturbance, set \(R(s)=0\). Since the disturbance enters after the gain \(40\), \[C(s)=G(s)\left[-40E(s)-D(s)\right],\qquad E(s)=-C(s)\] Hence, \[\frac{E(s)}{D(s)}=\frac{G(s)}{1+40G(s)}
=\frac{1}{s(1+5s)+40}\] For a unit-step disturbance, \[D(s)=\frac{1}{s},\qquad
E(s)=\frac{1}{s\left[s(1+5s)+40\right]}\] Using the final value theorem, \[e_{\mathrm{ss}}=\lim_{s\to0}sE(s)
=\lim_{s\to0}\frac{1}{s(1+5s)+40}
=\frac{1}{40}=0.025\] \[\boxed{\text{(D) }0.025}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a disturbance entering after the controller gain \(K\), \(\displaystyle \frac{E(s)}{D(s)}=\frac{G(s)}{1+KG(s)}\).</li><li>For a unit-step disturbance, use \(D(s)=1/s\) and the final value theorem.</li><li>Here \(K=40\), so the steady-state error is \(\displaystyle 1/K=1/40\).</li></ul></div>

From the Bode magnitude plot, <ul><li>Initial slope is \(-20\,\text{dB/decade}\) \(\Rightarrow\) one pole at the origin.</li><li>At \(\omega=2\,\text{rad/s}\), the slope changes from \(-20\) to \(0\,\text{dB/decade}\) \(\Rightarrow\) one zero at \(\omega_z=2\,\text{rad/s}.\)</li><li>At \(\omega=20\,\text{rad/s}\), the slope changes from \(0\) to \(-20\,\text{dB/decade}\) \(\Rightarrow\) one pole at \(\omega_p=20\,\text{rad/s}.\)</li></ul> Hence, the transfer function is of the form: \[G(s)=K\frac{\left(1+\dfrac{s}{2}\right)}
{s\left(1+\dfrac{s}{20}\right)}\] Between \(2&lt;\omega&lt;20,\) the magnitude plot is flat at \(20\,\text{dB}.\) In this frequency range, \(\left(1+\dfrac{s}{2}\right)\approx\dfrac{s}{2},\) and \(\left(1+\dfrac{s}{20}\right)\approx1.\) Therefore, \[|G(j\omega)|
\approx
K\frac{\omega/2}{\omega}
=\frac{K}{2}\] \[20\log\left(\frac{K}{2}\right)=20\] \[\frac{K}{2}=10\] \[K=20\] Hence, \[G(s)=\frac{20\left(1+\dfrac{s}{2}\right)}
{s\left(1+\dfrac{s}{20}\right)}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;\dfrac{20\left(\dfrac{s}{2}+1\right)}{s\left(\dfrac{s}{20}+1\right)}}\]

The transfer function of a PI controller is, \[G_c(s)=K_p\left(1+\frac{1}{T_is}\right), \qquad \text{where} ~~ T_i=\text{reset time}.\] Given, Proportional Band (PB) = \(50 \%\) and Reset time, \(T_i=0.2\,\text{s}.\) The proportional gain is related to the proportional band by, \[K_p=\frac{100}{\text{PB}}
=\frac{100}{50}
=2\] Also, \[\frac{1}{T_is}
=
\frac{1}{0.2s}
=
\frac{5}{s}
=
\frac{1}{s/5}\] Hence, \[G_c(s)
=
2\left(1+\frac{5}{s}\right)
=
2\left(1+\frac{1}{0.2s}\right)\] \[\boxed{(C)\;2\left(1+\dfrac{1}{0.2s}\right)}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Proportional gain and proportional band are related by, \(K_p=\dfrac{100}{\text{PB}(\%)}\)</li><li>The standard PI controller when reset time is given is, \(G_c(s)=K_p\left(1+\dfrac{1}{T_is}\right)\)</li></ul></div>
